{
  "result": "I keep thinking about the hour before sunrise, when the world has not yet decided what kind of day it intends to become. The sky is neither dark nor bright, and everything familiar looks slightly borrowed, as if houses and trees were returned during the night by forgetful hands. In that uncertain light, ordinary streets become honest. A bakery window glows, a bicycle leans against wet stone, and somewhere a kettle begins its small, determined argument with the quiet. I like beginnings because they make no promises they must keep.\n\nBy noon, the day has acquired opinions, deadlines, weather, and noise, but dawn still whispers that revision is possible. Perhaps that is why people fall in love on trains, write poems in kitchens, plant gardens after funerals, and forgive enemies while washing dishes. Hope rarely arrives wearing ceremonial clothes. It prefers sleeves rolled up, pockets full of seeds, and a pencil stub behind one ear. It sits beside us during ordinary labor and suggests, without insisting, that tomorrow need not be a photocopy of today.\n\nI have known despair, and it always sounded certain. Hope sounds less confident but more alive, like a bird testing a branch before trusting its weight. If I could keep anything from disappearing, it would not be youth, fame, certainty, or even time. I would keep the willingness to begin again badly, bravely, and without guarantees. That willingness has carried every artist, parent, lover, gardener, and broken heart safely toward morning. And it will again.",
